        "id": 35975,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-04-15T02:28:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"TheJayMann\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/thejaymann/48/2261_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> TheJayMann:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I find it interesting that people say St. Louis is close to Indianapolis. It is rather close when it comes to postage and shipping, but it's still rather far at about a 6 hour drive down I-70 either way.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Six hour drive? It's 242 miles. Even Google Maps thinks it is less than 4. With Rabit or Christopher driving, it may be closer to 3.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I lived in Hong Kong while I was finishing high school. I found it amusing to describe the (rather small) US state where I'd grown up as being between two cities. I'd use Philadelphia and Baltimore for people who were more than average in the know, but it more likely to be New York and Washington, DC. There was one person for whom the only city they could name in the US was Chicago, which I always found a bit odd. I mean, Chicago <em>IS</em> the third largest city in the country, but there are plenty of more well known cities (I think) that are smaller (DC, New Orleans, Boston, San Francisco, Miami, Dallas, for instance) not to mention the two larger cities, New York and Los Angeles.</p>",
        "post_number": 121,
